\p{\strong{8:22 AM}}
\hr
\p{A good example explanation of today's quote per ChatGPT.}
\example{ Imagine you spend years preparing for your dream job but don't get the offer.
The untrained response is:

\quotes{
"This shouldn't have happened. Everything is ruined."
}{Non-stoic}

The Stoic response is:
\quotes{
"I wanted this job, and I worked hard for it. But getting it was never completely in my control. I'll accept this outcome without bitterness and continue acting well."
}{Stoic}
Notice that the Stoic still preferred getting the job. Acceptance comes \strong{after} reality has spoken. Importantly, this is \strong{never} used as a reason to avoid working hard for the job in the first place.
}
\p{Finished my daily reading of [[hofstadterGodelEscherBach1999]]. Brief summary:}
\ul{
  \li{We had our first dialogue between the tortoise and Achilles.}
  \li{Zeno and his paradox were presented.}
  \li{I had a little fun playing around in the \code{MIU} system, and the interesting question of playing within vs. outside a system arose.}
  \li{He argued a distinction between humands and computers was that computers were able to effectively \em{turn off} their "thinking".}
  \example{Say, for example, in the \code{MIU} system we give the computer the list of transformations and tell it to continue until it shows that \code{U} is in the grammar. The computer will infinitely apply rules with no thought, where a human will quickly be able to tell this is impossible and will stop trying to do so.}
}

\p{\strong{10:39 AM}}
\hr
\question{What is the benefit of defining the complex CuTe layout algebra? Surely, it must buy us something to be worth the huge theoretical / mathematical overhead?}
\answer{The fact that it is a \em{complete} algebra, is useful in the sense that composing any two operations is guaranteed to remain a valid layout. This allows us to reason about layouts as the composition of better understood primitives instead of having to close-form expand the final expression. Additionally, these transformations can be applied at \em{compile time}, paying an upfront compilation time cost to get rid of any complex address calculation during runtime.}

\p{\strong{11:18 AM}}
\hr
\idea{One way to combine neuro + symbolic is to create a pure synthesizer that takes in example programs i.e. PyTorch and synthesizes the corresponding kernel i.e. \citek{wuMirageMultiLevelSuperoptimizer}. Then have another tool to synthesize the mega-kernel / composition like \citek{jinEventTensorUnified2026}.
\algorithm{
  The LLM would then operate as:
  \ul{
    \li{For each kernel,}
      \ol{
        \li{Provide PyTorch implementation of sub-kernel}
        \li{Run synthesizer and get output kernel}
        \ul{
          \li{Optionally, then optimize the synthesized kernel (if the modification is an easier task).}
      }
    }
    \li{Then compose the kernels with the mega-kernel tool.}
    \ul{
      \li{Optionally, then optimize the mega-kernel (if the modification is an easier task).}
    }
  }
}
}
\idea{Maybe the synthesizer also takes in weightings of operations. The LLM could adaptively update these weights based on the performance of synthesized programs.
\ul{
  \li{It is likely that the synthesizer is far too slow right now. (It seems \citek{wuMirageMultiLevelSuperoptimizer} takes on the order of hours). Making it faster (perhaps with Xinyu's help) could be a very useful thing then.}
}
}

